# Repository structure — design rationale

This page explains **why** {{ project_name }} is organized this way. It is not a
second path inventory. The marked Repository contract in the root
`README.md` is the sole placement authority; when this rationale
and that contract differ, the root contract wins.

## 1. Separate concerns by rate of change

Scientific repositories combine reusable algorithms, orchestration, large data,
and human communication. They age differently, so this template separates them:

- reusable library behavior changes deliberately and is typed/tested;
- pipeline wiring changes as analyses evolve and declares every dependency;
- data moves from immutable acquisition through disposable or reusable derived
  forms; and
- reporting source evolves under human review, while delivered versions freeze.

The point is not aesthetic symmetry. It lets a future reader tell whether a file
is source, evidence, cache, generated output, or a historical fact without
reverse-engineering how it was made.

## 2. One placement contract, many local explanations

The root README contains one marked, machine-checkable matrix for canonical
homes, storage, ownership, and lifecycle. Local READMEs may explain a directory's
practice, but do not redefine the matrix. Local `AGENTS.md` files add permissions
and safety constraints; parent rules continue to apply.

This asymmetry prevents two common failure modes: several partial directory
trees drifting apart, and an agent treating the nearest instruction file as
permission to ignore a repository-wide invariant. Placement is tested as part of
correctness because a scientifically correct file in the wrong lifecycle can be
lost, accidentally committed, or mistaken for evidence.

## 3. Data state follows reuse and irreversibility

Raw data is the only irreplaceable computational input and therefore stays
byte-immutable. Synthetic test fixtures are code fixtures, not pretend
acquisitions. Derived state is split by its downstream role:

- interim data is disposable glue within a pipeline;
- processed data is a reusable analysis interface; and
- results are terminal review or communication artifacts.

File extension does not decide the state. A Parquet dataset reused by another
analysis is processed data; a CSV summary used only in a report is a result.
Real raw payloads use {{ data_versioning }}. Processed payloads use it only when
their exact bytes must be shared or retained; generated results remain ignored.

## 4. Human records have distinct authorities

Bench execution, computational execution, research reasoning, and reporting are
different records and should not compete for authority.

{% if bench_record_authority == "eln" -%}
The ELN owns executed bench facts{% if bench_record_location %} at
`{{ bench_record_location }}`{% else %}; its location is intentionally marked
unconfigured until a person supplies it{% endif %}. The repository keeps reusable
protocols and stable pointers.
{% else -%}
This project deliberately uses human-created repository bench records under
`lab/records/`; agents cannot create or complete those physical-world facts.
{% endif %}

{% if use_lab_tracker -%}
Lab Tracker owns accepted questions, claims, decisions, dead ends, and pivots
{% if lab_tracker_project_id %}for project `{{ lab_tracker_project_id }}`{% else %}
after a person configures the project link{% endif %}. It stores semantic context
and artifact pointers, not raw/full figure bytes, ELN entries, task status, or
the run ledger.
{% else -%}
With Lab Tracker disabled, dated `notes/decisions/` files preserve
human-accepted research decisions. Ordinary notes remain scratch.
{% endif %}

Mutable reporting sources assemble evidence for an audience. A delivery freezes
exact payload bytes, figures, identifiers, and checksums only when a person
approves sending/presenting them.

The project hub (`docs/project-hub.md`) is deliberately none of these
authorities. It is a maintained communication projection: a person curates a
current summary and points each claim at its canonical record — the run
ledger, decision records, bench records, deliveries — instead of restating
them. Its "scientific content last reviewed" date is a human editorial fact,
distinct from Git and build metadata, which record edits and builds rather
than review. The hub updates by three-way merge on `copier update` like other
maintained files; when the page and a record disagree, the record wins.

## 5. Agents operate under inherited, artifact-based completion

The root `AGENTS.md` owns permissions. Nested files add local constraints and
cannot relax raw immutability, non-fabrication, human-gated durable mutations,
or the placement completion gate. `AGENTS.local.md` survives Copier updates so a
project can add stricter local rules without forking the template contract.

For scientific pipeline work, completion is demonstrated by inspectable
artifacts: validated inputs/schema/unit of analysis, deterministic exemplars,
per-unit and cohort QC, machine-readable metrics, explicit provenance,
reproducible commands, invariant tests, and an end-to-end smoke run. Pure
numerical kernels target at least 90% coverage; the repository-wide gate may be
lower because adapters and generated surfaces have different testing shapes.

## 6. Theme is source configuration, not copied styling

Colors, typography, and figure dimensions are declared once in `conf/theme/`.
Library functions validate/interpret the theme; a pipeline stage renders
consumer-specific Matplotlib and TeX artifacts. Generated theme files are never
edited by hand.

This preserves consistent light/print and dark/screen variants without keeping
two manually divergent plot implementations. Accessibility and physical figure
dimensions are scientific communication parameters and therefore reviewed as
configuration.

## 7. One declarative DAG owns computation

Snakemake declares causality from catalogued inputs and configuration to
processed data, QC, figures, tables, logs, and current provenance. Rules state
all dependencies; thin adapters translate Snakemake objects into calls to tested
library behavior. Hidden writes and notebook dependencies would make staleness
undetectable, so they are prohibited.

Configuration is split by ownership rather than convenience:

- `conf/config.yaml` owns scientific choices;
- `conf/catalog.yaml` owns logical dataset bindings;
- `workflow/config.yaml` owns topology/selectors; and
- workflow profiles own executor/scheduler resources.

A value should appear in only one of those authorities.

## 8. Figures are either regenerable outputs or authored inputs

A data-derived plot is a build target. Review changes are implemented in code or
configuration and rerun. A schematic that a person must redraw is an authored
input with editable source and source metadata. Small authored assets fit in
Git; larger or frequently changing binaries use {{ data_versioning }} at the
same logical path.

A composite assembled in code remains regenerable even if it reads an authored
panel. A hand-assembled composite is deliberately non-regenerable and therefore
becomes an authored asset. Exact copies enter versioned history only when a
reporting delivery is frozen.

## 9. Environments and secrets are explicit

The project environment is local to the repository, dependencies are declared
and lockable, and HPC execution is routed through a profile{% if use_apptainer %}
with optional Apptainer isolation{% endif %}. Runtime secrets come from
environment variables or an ignored `.env`; configuration and documentation
contain names and examples, never credentials.

Environment identity in provenance must describe the realized runtime rather
than pretend a lock or container was used when it was not. Git and DVC identities
are retrieved from their tools, including dirty state.

## 10. Metadata and provenance separate current from durable truth

Validated manifests connect stable scientific identifiers to raw files. They are
small Git-tracked facts, while payload bytes remain in the data layer. Invalid or
unregistered inputs fail loudly instead of becoming implicit sample discovery.

The pipeline writes a complete current-run manifest to
`results/provenance.json`. Human-approved registration serializes writers,
publishes the complete immutable archive under
`metadata/provenance/<execution_id>.json` first, and then atomically rewrites the
execution index. This is not a two-file atomic transaction: an idempotent retry
completes an archive-only state after an interrupted ledger write. A human
interpretation entry then references that registered execution. Keeping
generation, registration, and interpretation separate prevents a routine
pipeline run from silently becoming a permanent scientific claim.

